00:00:06.217 --> 00:00:08.300
DAMIAN LAWLOR: We have
lots to cover this morning.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.250
We've also got some really,
really great stories

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.375
from customers who are
going to join us up onstage.

00:00:12.375 --> 00:00:15.950
So I'm going to speak very
quickly, not just because I'm

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:18.710
Irish, but because I want to get
off the stage as fast as I can

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.575
and welcome some
more of my colleagues

00:00:20.575 --> 00:00:21.950
and some of those
great customers

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:24.110
up to share their
experiences with you.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.982
So a little bit about myself.

00:00:26.982 --> 00:00:29.190
I'll just give you a little
kind of personal journey.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.890
I'm 11 years in Google.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:35.150
I spent two years running
the Publisher side in Europe,

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.610
then I moved across after
the DoubleClick acquisition

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.213
and ran DoubleClick for
six years in Europem

00:00:40.213 --> 00:00:42.380
and I've been in Cloud for
about three and 1/2 years

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:42.963
at this stage.

00:00:42.963 --> 00:00:47.360
And I was really attracted
to the transformation

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.757
that was taking place in people
moving from an on-premise world

00:00:50.757 --> 00:00:52.340
into a cloud world
and what that could

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.580
mean for helping companies
with their own digital

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:56.630
transformation.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.260
But I was particularly
interested,

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.100
coming out of the ad tech world,
about what cloud computing

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.220
could do to really help
people with their marketing

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.450
and how they could interact
with their own customers.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.190
So I saw it as a compelling
transformation story

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:13.280
as the consumer shift to
mobile, which we all saw,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.810
or the shift to
programmatic advertising.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.243
So if you think of those two big
shifts, another really, really

00:01:19.243 --> 00:01:21.410
large shift that we're
seeing, and I love this quote

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:25.150
from Gartner, is,
this transformation

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:29.390
of how many marketers see that
they'll be "competing mostly

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.910
or completely on the basis
of customer experience in two

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:32.920
years' time."

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.400
The quote's from 2017.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.920
That means that it should
actually be happening now.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.903
So just a quick question
to the audience.

00:01:39.903 --> 00:01:41.320
The marketers who
are in the room,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.322
are you now mostly or
completely competing

00:01:44.322 --> 00:01:46.530
on the basis of customer
experience or not quite yet?

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:47.780
So yes?

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.190
OK.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:52.300
That's pretty interesting.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.177
And I think one of
the reasons why--

00:01:55.177 --> 00:01:56.760
nothing's really
changed in marketing.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.090
Marketers are still looking
to acquire, retain, and grow

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.520
customers.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.010
And they want to put the
customer in the center

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.732
and focus on
customer engagement,

00:02:06.732 --> 00:02:08.190
and really want to
focus on what we

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:12.220
call creating delightful
customer experiences.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:17.070
And that's going to help you
to predict how much people will

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.485
spend in the future,
predict is your customer,

00:02:19.485 --> 00:02:22.740
is she likely to spend again,
how do we find others like her

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.250
at scale, et cetera.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.480
So you've got all
of these touchpoints

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.230
from your customers,
all of this fantastic

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:32.760
data you're collecting, but
a lot of that data is sitting

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.960
in either technological silos
or in organizational silos

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.800
in your organization.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.222
And so that makes it very
hard to get a complete view

00:02:43.222 --> 00:02:43.930
of your customer.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.458
So your email marketing team
may have fantastic insights

00:02:46.458 --> 00:02:48.000
in your customers,
but is not sharing

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.290
that with your search team.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.630
And you may not be getting
feedback back fast enough

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:55.605
to your product development or
your customer service teams.

00:02:55.605 --> 00:02:58.800
And you may also
be working with--

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.050
I know there's a stat out
there that on average people

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.970
use 13 different
marketing technologies.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:06.000
But in fact, even
somebody today told me

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.550
that, actually, the
different tools that collect

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.903
data that sit in silos
numbers in the thousands

00:03:10.903 --> 00:03:12.570
in their organization,
and how difficult

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.760
it is to actually pull
all that data together.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.420
So this is what
really interested me.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:24.240
So when I started off in Cloud,
I did a global round trip, just

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.960
going and meeting as many
CMOs and C-suite CEOs,

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.720
and pretty much asking them
the same question, which was,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.060
how satisfied are you
with your own ability

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.720
to understand your own
data on your own customers?

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.810
And I always tell
the joke that I

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.270
have 100% record
of people saying

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:43.975
they are very dissatisfied.

00:03:43.975 --> 00:03:45.600
And the joke is that
there was actually

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.010
one CMO who told me that
they were very satisfied

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.370
with the amount of the
value and insight they

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:54.820
got from their own data.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:57.540
So that kind of
broke my 100% record.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.010
But then afterwards, they
came back up to me and said,

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:01.710
I only said that because
I was in front of my team,

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:03.210
and I didn't want
to embarrass them.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.090
In fact, we're really unhappy
with how much value we can get

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:07.460
out of our own customer data.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.960
So what that actually means is
that the statistic behind me,

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:12.540
where 13% of
organizations say they're

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.790
making the most of their
available customer data

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:19.019
is exactly 13% more than I'd
met in actual discussions

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:21.310
with all of you.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.250
And what I find
really interesting

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:28.200
is that the more sophisticated
the enterprise that we work

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.092
with get, the less
satisfied they

00:04:30.092 --> 00:04:31.800
become with the amount
of value that they

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.720
can get out of their
own data, because it

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:34.950
feeds their requirement.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.470
If they do start seeing
how fantastic insights

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.240
they can pull out
of their own data,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:41.115
then they want to get
more and more insights.

00:04:41.115 --> 00:04:43.800
They want to start using
machine model, learning models.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.050
They want to make it faster
to get to those insights.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.092
They want to be able to
use smarter technologies.

00:04:48.092 --> 00:04:49.920
And they want to make
it easier for all

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.420
of the people in
their organizations

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.350
to be able to get to
those insights quickly.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.560
So this is where Cloud
for Marketing comes in.

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.440
Our overall vision is to
take what is typically,

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.290
for a lot of people, this linear
model of the data journey--

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.620
collecting your data,
transforming it, analyzing it,

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:09.720
visualizing it,
and activating it--

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.890
and offering faster, simpler,
and smarter solutions

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.900
for you to create these
delightful customer

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.200
experiences from your data and
from your marketing technology,

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:23.130
and ultimately, with the
power of cloud computing,

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.070
to turn that more linear
model into a virtuous circle.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:29.280
Because, obviously, as you
activate these campaigns,

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.950
as you activate these
delightful customer experiences,

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:35.048
then customers react
and supply more data

00:05:35.048 --> 00:05:37.590
about what their preferences
are and what they want from you.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:41.250
And 63% of consumers
say they want

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.360
brands to understand
them better and use

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:45.960
the data from their
purchasing history

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.780
to provide them with a
more personalized solution.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.050
And what we're here to
do is to bring technology

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:55.710
to bear on that to enable
you to do that more

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:57.400
and more with your customers.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:58.890
So to go into more
detail about how

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:01.030
