00:30:50.810 --> 00:30:53.560
if you didn't know that.

00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:55.760
And also the women's
brand, Aerie.

00:30:55.760 --> 00:30:57.970
So American Eagle is
all about empowering

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:00.970
youth and individuality.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:04.870
Aerie is really about
celebrating real beauty

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:06.720
in young women.

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:10.110
So I say these
pronouns we and us,

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:12.000
because the reality
is I'm a vice

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:15.360
president of something-or-other
at a $4 billion company.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:19.070
I didn't do this work, my
team did all this work.

00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:19.570
OK.

00:31:19.570 --> 00:31:21.280
So you heard a lot
about you've got

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:22.970
to understand who
your customers are,

00:31:22.970 --> 00:31:24.720
what they want, what
are their intentions,

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:26.710
how do you use machine
learning and technology,

00:31:26.710 --> 00:31:29.440
and so on, to make
sure you're there

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:31.420
when they want you to be there?

00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:34.315
And I think for us, it comes
down to listening, right?

00:31:34.315 --> 00:31:36.190
You've got to be there
listening and engaging

00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:39.370
in a meaningful conversation
with your customer, every time,

00:31:39.370 --> 00:31:41.525
when they're ready to
engage in that conversation.

00:31:41.525 --> 00:31:43.150
So if you don't know
this Ramones song,

00:31:43.150 --> 00:31:45.100
the next line is, you
gotta learn to listen

00:31:45.100 --> 00:31:46.540
or you're going to get burned.

00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:49.900
So I think, for us, it's really
about listening to our customer

00:31:49.900 --> 00:31:53.260
and understanding who they
are at the moment when they're

00:31:53.260 --> 00:31:55.210
signaling intent.

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:58.540
So this is a Googleized
representation

00:31:58.540 --> 00:32:02.680
of a high level picture
of about 10 people's

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:04.490
five years worth of work.

00:32:04.490 --> 00:32:06.850
We have this really
comprehensive customer journey

00:32:06.850 --> 00:32:10.480
map that our team has done
that I can't share with you.

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:11.830
It's terribly proprietary.

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:14.980
But you can understand
the idea is,

00:32:14.980 --> 00:32:16.660
as a customer's
engaging with us,

00:32:16.660 --> 00:32:19.400
they go through these
phases of engagement,

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:21.710
whether it's from
influences to consideration

00:32:21.710 --> 00:32:25.720
into in-store digital browsing,
purchasing, usage, returns,

00:32:25.720 --> 00:32:27.620
and so on.

00:32:27.620 --> 00:32:30.320
And you know, the CX
team, like I said,

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:33.260
did all of this incredible
work mapping out the journey,

00:32:33.260 --> 00:32:37.440
doing ethnographic research,
talking to customers,

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:40.340
understanding what it is
like to be that customer

00:32:40.340 --> 00:32:42.380
when they're engaging
with our brands.

00:32:42.380 --> 00:32:45.470
And then you see the little
gray call-out box there.

00:32:45.470 --> 00:32:48.440
That's really the call-out
then to the technology team,

00:32:48.440 --> 00:32:52.010
or my team in particular, in
that our job is to understand

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:55.730
and measure what's happening
for 20 million people at a time

00:32:55.730 --> 00:32:58.440
as they move through
this customer journey.

00:32:58.440 --> 00:32:59.380
So it's important.

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:00.880
You know, we're a
business, so we've

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:03.470
got to tie this back to what
we do as a business, right?

00:33:03.470 --> 00:33:06.385
So we have OKRs and
metrics in place

00:33:06.385 --> 00:33:07.760
that help us
measure this journey

00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:09.380
and understand
what's important--

00:33:09.380 --> 00:33:12.320
so things like conversion,
revenue per visitor,

00:33:12.320 --> 00:33:13.220
average order volume.

00:33:13.220 --> 00:33:16.040
Those are things that are
important to online retailers--

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:17.300
Ulta guys, right?

00:33:17.300 --> 00:33:18.560
I hope.

00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:21.110
And then we put metrics
and goals in place.

00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:23.810
So we want to move
conversion by 2%, let's say.

00:33:23.810 --> 00:33:29.030
We want to increase average
order volume to $50 and so on.

00:33:29.030 --> 00:33:31.090
And those are
quantifiable things

00:33:31.090 --> 00:33:32.770
that we can understand
as we measure

00:33:32.770 --> 00:33:35.140
customer interaction, how
they shop, what they buy,

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:36.707
what they browse, and so on.

00:33:36.707 --> 00:33:39.040
But then the last part, which
can be the hardest piece--

00:33:39.040 --> 00:33:40.330
and I'm going to talk a little
bit about what we're doing

00:33:40.330 --> 00:33:41.050
there--

00:33:41.050 --> 00:33:42.883
is how do customers
feel about it when

00:33:42.883 --> 00:33:44.050
they're interacting with us?

00:33:44.050 --> 00:33:45.850
How does it feel
to be our customer?

00:33:45.850 --> 00:33:47.390
Not how many of them are there?

00:33:47.390 --> 00:33:48.443
What do they buy?

00:33:48.443 --> 00:33:50.110
What did they buy
with this other stuff,

00:33:50.110 --> 00:33:52.115
and how can I make them
buy more of that stuff?

00:33:52.115 --> 00:33:52.990
But how do they feel?

00:33:52.990 --> 00:33:54.910
What do they say?

00:33:54.910 --> 00:33:56.950
But not one at a time,
how did they feel

00:33:56.950 --> 00:33:59.770
and what did they say 20 million
at a time, all of the time,

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:00.730
every time?

00:34:00.730 --> 00:34:02.800
And how can I be there
listening as they're

00:34:02.800 --> 00:34:05.630
telling me those things?

00:34:05.630 --> 00:34:07.710
So two themes I'm
going to talk about.

00:34:07.710 --> 00:34:09.739
One is personalization.

00:34:09.739 --> 00:34:11.420
So similar to what
the Ulta guys said,

00:34:11.420 --> 00:34:14.239
we have a pretty comprehensive
personalization program

00:34:14.239 --> 00:34:15.252
under way.

00:34:15.252 --> 00:34:17.210
We've got a lot of those
capabilities built out

00:34:17.210 --> 00:34:20.570
on the Google Cloud, thanks
to my awesome team again.

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:22.820
But again, we're not just
doing personalization

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:23.900
for personalization.

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:26.810
And quite frankly, we're not
even just doing personalization

00:34:26.810 --> 00:34:28.580
to be there for
the customer when

00:34:28.580 --> 00:34:29.798
they want us to be and so on.

00:34:29.798 --> 00:34:31.340
We are running a
business, so we want

00:34:31.340 --> 00:34:33.007
to make sure we're
doing personalization

00:34:33.007 --> 00:34:35.179
in a way that ties
back to the things

00:34:35.179 --> 00:34:37.130
that our business cares about.

00:34:37.130 --> 00:34:39.770
So calling out four
particular examples--

00:34:39.770 --> 00:34:43.580
when we engage customers
in one-to-one experiences,

00:34:43.580 --> 00:34:45.170
we see significant
revenue impact.

00:34:45.170 --> 00:34:48.440
I think the number there
is 21%, or something

00:34:48.440 --> 00:34:50.120
like that, of our
customers that don't

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:52.520
interact with our
personalization tactics.

00:34:52.520 --> 00:34:54.020
Similarly, on the
conversion side,

00:34:54.020 --> 00:34:57.920
we see like 4x conversion
over customers that don't

00:34:57.920 --> 00:34:59.690
interact with personalization.

00:34:59.690 --> 00:35:03.020
So this might be a sales
pitch for personalization.

00:35:03.020 --> 00:35:05.857
Increased units and outfitting--
so we're a fashion retailer.

00:35:05.857 --> 00:35:07.940
We sell clothes, so we
want to make sure customers

00:35:07.940 --> 00:35:09.140
are outfitting themselves.

00:35:09.140 --> 00:35:11.810
They're not just coming for
one particular item or another.

00:35:11.810 --> 00:35:14.510
But we want them to
understand the outfitting

00:35:14.510 --> 00:35:16.530
intention of our merchants.

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:17.610
And so we see--

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:21.848
I forget there-- it's 18%
or 19% increase in the units

00:35:21.848 --> 00:35:23.390
the customers are
buying when they're

00:35:23.390 --> 00:35:26.160
interacting with our
personalization tactics.

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:29.720
And then similarly, for higher
revenue and margin per sale.

00:35:29.720 --> 00:35:33.370
So the point is that the
team is doing amazing work,

00:35:33.370 --> 00:35:34.490
and it's having an impact.

00:35:34.490 --> 00:35:37.520
And it's technically interesting
and invigorating, I think,

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:39.800
to come to work every day
and work on this stuff.

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:42.950
Then we can go talk to the board
of directors about the impact

00:35:42.950 --> 00:35:45.830
we're having in 4x
conversion and 28%,

00:35:45.830 --> 00:35:48.080
or whatever it is, increase
in average order volume.

00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:50.930
Those are meaningful numbers
for the board of a $4 billion

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:53.770
public company.

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:55.120
So how do we do that?

00:35:55.120 --> 00:35:56.370
This is just a quick overview.

00:35:56.370 --> 00:35:58.790
Again, my team's here, if you
want to ask them questions.

00:35:58.790 --> 00:36:00.720
But you know, we have
the problem that Damian

00:36:00.720 --> 00:36:01.970
talked about at the beginning.

00:36:01.970 --> 00:36:03.150
We've got all this data.

00:36:03.150 --> 00:36:05.860
It doesn't natively
exist in Google.

00:36:05.860 --> 00:36:10.030
So on the left,
your left, we've got

00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:11.910
to get all this data
in there to personalize

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:12.840
people's experiences.

00:36:12.840 --> 00:36:16.950
I've got to have customer data,
product data, browse data--

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:21.030
tf-idf is an awesome
recommendation based on text

00:36:21.030 --> 00:36:23.580
distance frequency for
items in our catalog

00:36:23.580 --> 00:36:25.872
and our assortment--

00:36:25.872 --> 00:36:27.330
and all this customer
data in there

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:30.630
so that we can understand who's
looking for what, what they

00:36:30.630 --> 00:36:34.350
might be interested in adjacent
to what they are browsing

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:35.490
at the time, right?

00:36:35.490 --> 00:36:38.913
So we put it through
this pipeline on Google,

00:36:38.913 --> 00:36:41.580
apply some machine learning-- we
have this robust recommendation

00:36:41.580 --> 00:36:43.200
engine that we built--

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:46.050
and serve those out in real-time
in a recommendation service

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:48.480
that we built that runs an
app engine that our site can

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:50.650
call in real-time.

00:36:50.650 --> 00:36:52.290
I don't have a
similar architecture,

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:54.840
but we also have a
real-time pricing and offer

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:56.910
service that we run similarly.

00:36:56.910 --> 00:36:58.940
So our customer can
come to the site

00:36:58.940 --> 00:37:01.320
and, based on segmentation
work that we do,

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:03.990
I could make pricing and offer
recommendations or promotion

00:37:03.990 --> 00:37:05.580
recommendations
to that customer,

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:08.000
based on, you can
imagine, similar logic.

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:14.960
OK so voice of the
customer-- so this is about,

00:37:14.960 --> 00:37:16.500
how does it feel
to be our customer?

00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:18.530
And how do I measure that?

00:37:18.530 --> 00:37:20.838
So a lot of this gets back
to sentiment analysis,

00:37:20.838 --> 00:37:21.630
as you can imagine.

00:37:21.630 --> 00:37:23.270
But again, we're
trying to tie this

00:37:23.270 --> 00:37:25.380
back to running a business.

00:37:25.380 --> 00:37:28.790
So our vision here
is putting content

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:30.860
about what customers are
saying and how they're

00:37:30.860 --> 00:37:33.740
feeling about our products
and our experiences

00:37:33.740 --> 00:37:35.720
into the hands of the
people in our business

00:37:35.720 --> 00:37:37.910
that are responsible
for those things.

00:37:37.910 --> 00:37:40.310
So you can imagine our
sourcing and manufacturing

00:37:40.310 --> 00:37:42.500
team really care if
our jeans are smelly,

00:37:42.500 --> 00:37:45.390
if they're bleeding, if they're
tearing out at the seams,

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:48.338
if the fabric is pilly,
those kinds of things.

00:37:48.338 --> 00:37:50.380
And you can get anecdotal
one off here and there.

00:37:50.380 --> 00:37:52.120
A customer said this,
customer said that.

00:37:52.120 --> 00:37:54.550
We got 10 returns in
the distribution center

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:56.260
because of this thing.

00:37:56.260 --> 00:37:59.110
But through the service
that we're building here,

00:37:59.110 --> 00:38:01.575
we can put dashboards
in front of those teams

00:38:01.575 --> 00:38:03.700
and measure every interaction
that our customer has

00:38:03.700 --> 00:38:06.280
and everything they say
every time about how

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:08.930
they're feeling about
every one of our products.

00:38:08.930 --> 00:38:12.160
So I think that's going to be
pretty powerful for that team,

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:14.285
similarly, for the
merchant teams.

00:38:14.285 --> 00:38:16.660
So the job, if you don't know
the job, of retail merchant

00:38:16.660 --> 00:38:19.000
is to figure out what
clothes to put together

00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:20.790
into an assortment,
how those things ought

00:38:20.790 --> 00:38:22.300
to go together at scale.

00:38:22.300 --> 00:38:24.380
We sell 60,000
different units a year,

00:38:24.380 --> 00:38:26.410
so that's a big
problem to solve.

00:38:26.410 --> 00:38:28.263
And then anticipate
what they think

00:38:28.263 --> 00:38:29.680
people are going
to like, and then

00:38:29.680 --> 00:38:31.930
try to read that through the
season to understand what

00:38:31.930 --> 00:38:34.000
people are feeling
about the products

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:35.920
that they put out there
in their assortment.

00:38:35.920 --> 00:38:40.060
And sometimes, things move
faster and slower than they

00:38:40.060 --> 00:38:41.770
thought they were going to.

00:38:41.770 --> 00:38:44.770
And we can use sentiment
analysis or our voice

00:38:44.770 --> 00:38:47.650
of the customer engine here to
help them understand not just

00:38:47.650 --> 00:38:50.517
this thing's moving slow,
market down, drive-through

00:38:50.517 --> 00:38:51.100
the inventory.

00:38:51.100 --> 00:38:52.405
That's kind of retail 101.

00:38:52.405 --> 00:38:55.030
But maybe the things are moving
slow because of something else.

00:38:55.030 --> 00:38:57.970
And people are saying something
about the products, right?

00:38:57.970 --> 00:38:59.750
And so we ran an
example last year

00:38:59.750 --> 00:39:02.260
where it was around
this time of year.

00:39:02.260 --> 00:39:05.008
And our men's shorts were
sort of selling slow.

00:39:05.008 --> 00:39:07.300
And the merchants felt really
good about the assortment

00:39:07.300 --> 00:39:09.940
they had put together, but
it was just selling slow.

00:39:09.940 --> 00:39:11.440
And in a traditional
retailer, you'd

00:39:11.440 --> 00:39:12.760
think, well, OK,
it's selling slow.

00:39:12.760 --> 00:39:14.135
We've got to move
this inventory.

00:39:14.135 --> 00:39:14.680
Mark it down.

00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:15.472
Get it out of here.

00:39:15.472 --> 00:39:17.710
Take a hit on margin and
suffer for that, right?

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:19.990
But through looking at
our voice of the customer,

00:39:19.990 --> 00:39:22.900
we were able to see that,
by and large, customers

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:25.210
were feeling pretty good
about the assortment.

00:39:25.210 --> 00:39:27.100
They were saying
positive things.

00:39:27.100 --> 00:39:28.810
They liked how the
assortment looked.

00:39:28.810 --> 00:39:31.330
They liked how the
product felt, right?

00:39:31.330 --> 00:39:32.630
But it just wasn't selling.

00:39:32.630 --> 00:39:35.260
So we were able to kind of hold
the line with some confidence

00:39:35.260 --> 00:39:36.620
to say, no, people like this.

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:38.328
And it's not just,
hey, do you like this?

00:39:38.328 --> 00:39:40.755
It's hey, everybody, 20 million
of you at the same time,

00:39:40.755 --> 00:39:42.130
how do you feel
about this stuff?

00:39:42.130 --> 00:39:42.670
Do you like it?

00:39:42.670 --> 00:39:44.128
And the customers
are saying, yeah,

00:39:44.128 --> 00:39:45.620
we kind of like this stuff.

00:39:45.620 --> 00:39:48.735
So we kind of were able to hold
the line in terms of markdowns.

00:39:48.735 --> 00:39:50.110
And then by the
way, the business

00:39:50.110 --> 00:39:51.110
came back and did great.

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:53.410
So it was just kind
of one other indicator

00:39:53.410 --> 00:39:55.480
to help us have some
confidence in the assortment

00:39:55.480 --> 00:39:56.438
that we'd put together.

00:39:58.750 --> 00:39:59.250
OK.

00:39:59.250 --> 00:40:02.390
So similarly, the
pipeline, again--

00:40:02.390 --> 00:40:04.670
to Damian's point at
the very beginning--

00:40:04.670 --> 00:40:07.160
the problem is that I
have all of this data.

00:40:07.160 --> 00:40:10.280
It doesn't natively
exist on Google, right?

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:11.480
I have product reviews.

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:12.650
I have purchase surveys.

00:40:12.650 --> 00:40:13.820
We have social media data.

00:40:13.820 --> 00:40:16.020
We have data from
our call center.

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:17.750
From the call center,
we have chats.

00:40:17.750 --> 00:40:18.410
We have calls.

00:40:18.410 --> 00:40:19.280
We have emails.

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:22.340
There's a lot of things that
people are saying, right?

00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:24.913
Returns data-- there's
surveys on the returns.

00:40:24.913 --> 00:40:26.330
We have to bring
all of that stuff

00:40:26.330 --> 00:40:28.280
together and amalgamate
it into one thing,

00:40:28.280 --> 00:40:30.680
so I can understand
with one taxonomy, what

00:40:30.680 --> 00:40:31.880
are people saying?

00:40:31.880 --> 00:40:32.840
And how do they feel?

00:40:32.840 --> 00:40:35.132
And that's where this kind
of sentiment analysis engine

00:40:35.132 --> 00:40:35.888
lays on top.

00:40:35.888 --> 00:40:38.430
And then for now, we're kind of
natively building in Tableau.

00:40:38.430 --> 00:40:40.730
If there's a Tableau
person in here, shout-out.

00:40:40.730 --> 00:40:44.125
Kind of natively building a
little app interface in Tableau

00:40:44.125 --> 00:40:46.250
so that our merchant teams
and some of the business

00:40:46.250 --> 00:40:53.010
folks I talked about can
interact with that data.

00:40:53.010 --> 00:40:58.460
OK, I wanted to talk about
a voice of a real customer.

00:40:58.460 --> 00:41:02.610
You know, Aerie was the
first brand to not retouch,

00:41:02.610 --> 00:41:05.460
to not airbrush our models.

00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:08.820
And we've kind of taken that one
step further in the past year,

00:41:08.820 --> 00:41:13.357
and we've actually
recruited real women

00:41:13.357 --> 00:41:15.690
to be a part of our marketing
and advertising campaigns.

00:41:15.690 --> 00:41:16.740
And if you go to
our website and you

00:41:16.740 --> 00:41:19.350
want to see how we merchandise
our products on the website,

00:41:19.350 --> 00:41:22.820
those are all real women
wearing our clothes.

00:41:22.820 --> 00:41:25.360
And so you can see in
this particular example--

00:41:25.360 --> 00:41:27.390
I think there's a laser here--

00:41:27.390 --> 00:41:29.310
so this young woman
was on our site.

00:41:29.310 --> 00:41:31.120
And she had had a
heart transplant.

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:33.010
So she has one of
these big zipper scars

00:41:33.010 --> 00:41:34.960
down the middle of her chest.

00:41:34.960 --> 00:41:37.450
And we just so happened to
have another young woman

00:41:37.450 --> 00:41:41.260
in a similar condition who
was merchant modeling some

00:41:41.260 --> 00:41:43.573
of our clothes on the website.

00:41:43.573 --> 00:41:45.240
And you can read for
yourselves, but you

00:41:45.240 --> 00:41:48.120
can see that something
along the lines of she

00:41:48.120 --> 00:41:50.760
went to bed feeling a little
more comfortable with herself

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:53.500
that night, based on
what we were doing.

00:41:53.500 --> 00:41:57.240
So you know, it's not just
machine learning, and AI,

00:41:57.240 --> 00:41:58.980
and reasoning over
people's intent to try

00:41:58.980 --> 00:42:00.340
to sell them more stuff.

00:42:00.340 --> 00:42:00.840
No.

00:42:00.840 --> 00:42:03.660
I think the reality is
that if you do it right,

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:05.160
you're listening
to your customers,

00:42:05.160 --> 00:42:07.618
you're paying attention to what
they say and how they feel,

00:42:07.618 --> 00:42:10.032
and you can have a real
impact on people's lives.

00:42:10.032 --> 00:42:11.490
BIREN KALARIA: Last
up today, we've

00:42:11.490 --> 00:42:15.120
got Tim Booher, who is the
Global Analytics Lead and Chief

00:42:15.120 --> 00:42:17.750
Information Security Officer
from Colgate-Palmolive.

00:42:17.750 --> 00:42:18.960
Tim, please come on stage.

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:19.460
Thank you.

00:42:22.074 --> 00:42:24.494
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:24.494 --> 00:42:26.073
TIM BOOHER: Great.

00:42:26.073 --> 00:42:27.490
Thanks for the
opportunity to tell

00:42:27.490 --> 00:42:31.450
the story of how, at Colgate,
we're using this technology

00:42:31.450 --> 00:42:35.620
to really pull together a bunch
of different data sources,

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:38.205
empower our users,
and have a lot of fun.

00:42:38.205 --> 00:42:40.330
For those of you that don't
know Colgate-Palmolive,

00:42:40.330 --> 00:42:44.230
we have over 3 and 1/2 billion
customers for different product

00:42:44.230 --> 00:42:47.740
lines, home care, pet care,
personal care, and oral care,

00:42:47.740 --> 00:42:49.870
which is the majority
of our business.

00:42:49.870 --> 00:42:51.760
Very global country,
with the majority

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:55.360
of our sales outside
the United States.

00:42:55.360 --> 00:42:57.640
Very established in
developing countries.

00:42:57.640 --> 00:43:00.970
And you can imagine that
makes a big challenge for us

00:43:00.970 --> 00:43:05.260
to understand, with such a
diverse group of customers,

00:43:05.260 --> 00:43:07.390
what they think about our
products, what they want,

00:43:07.390 --> 00:43:10.330
and how they respond to
all of our creative work

00:43:10.330 --> 00:43:12.010
that we're doing.

00:43:12.010 --> 00:43:14.500
My background before
coming into Colgate

00:43:14.500 --> 00:43:15.890
was with the US government.

00:43:15.890 --> 00:43:18.760
I had the opportunity to
run part of DARPA's machine

00:43:18.760 --> 00:43:20.145
learning portfolio.

00:43:20.145 --> 00:43:22.270
Whether it was fighting
human trafficking networks,

00:43:22.270 --> 00:43:24.670
using AI to infiltrate
terrorist networks

00:43:24.670 --> 00:43:30.670
and find radicalization,
or trying to put terrorists

00:43:30.670 --> 00:43:34.060
behind bars before
they could act,

00:43:34.060 --> 00:43:36.320
all that required us going
out and getting data.

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:38.230
We didn't look for a
tool that provided that.

00:43:38.230 --> 00:43:40.270
We found the smartest
people we could.

00:43:40.270 --> 00:43:42.250
We built everything
using open source,

00:43:42.250 --> 00:43:44.990
and we had all the computing
power that we wanted.

00:43:44.990 --> 00:43:47.872
I also had hundreds of people
and millions a year to do it.

00:43:47.872 --> 00:43:50.080
When you're in a commercial
construct, what's amazing

00:43:50.080 --> 00:43:54.460
to me is everything that that
I got mostly from organizations

00:43:54.460 --> 00:43:56.320
like MIT that built
everything in C

00:43:56.320 --> 00:44:00.130
and were able to do entity
extraction and all this work,

00:44:00.130 --> 00:44:02.830
you can get instantly
now on Google's cloud.

00:44:02.830 --> 00:44:04.630
And that's incredibly
exciting to me,

00:44:04.630 --> 00:44:07.610
because we can get a
lot done very quickly.

00:44:07.610 --> 00:44:10.060
In this case, we looked
at consumer sentiment

00:44:10.060 --> 00:44:11.170
as a starting point.

00:44:11.170 --> 00:44:12.670
And it really opened
the door for us

00:44:12.670 --> 00:44:15.140
to do a lot of other
exciting things.

00:44:15.140 --> 00:44:17.350
The first experiment
we did with Google

00:44:17.350 --> 00:44:19.780
is we looked at several
hundred thousand reviews

00:44:19.780 --> 00:44:22.120
of our customer's
products online, then

00:44:22.120 --> 00:44:23.800
wrote a bunch of
Python glue code

00:44:23.800 --> 00:44:26.020
to fit together
all the master data

00:44:26.020 --> 00:44:29.950
and add some structure to
the data that was out there,

00:44:29.950 --> 00:44:31.960
and then used Google's
Natural Language API.

00:44:31.960 --> 00:44:33.502
And this still floors
me a little bit

00:44:33.502 --> 00:44:35.980
because I had hundreds of
people building capability

00:44:35.980 --> 00:44:38.860
that got about half
of what this has.

00:44:38.860 --> 00:44:41.090
And we were doing some
really awesome stuff with it.

00:44:41.090 --> 00:44:43.330
But here, I can
use a smaller team,

00:44:43.330 --> 00:44:46.270
Cloud infrastructure,
scale up what we need,

00:44:46.270 --> 00:44:49.630
and get a lot of work
done more quickly.

00:44:49.630 --> 00:44:52.390
What's amazing about
this is entity extraction

00:44:52.390 --> 00:44:54.730
used to be one of our hardest,
most technical problems.

00:44:54.730 --> 00:44:58.240
It comes out of the box,
along with sentiment score,

00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:00.700
salience of how relevant,
and even magnitude

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:03.250
of feeling on different
topics that are there.

00:45:03.250 --> 00:45:04.648
Once we can do
entity extraction,

00:45:04.648 --> 00:45:06.190
that's where it gets
really exciting.

00:45:06.190 --> 00:45:08.197
Because even when we
start with reviews,

00:45:08.197 --> 00:45:10.780
depending on the business user--
and there are eight different

00:45:10.780 --> 00:45:12.670
business users that are
now using this data,

00:45:12.670 --> 00:45:14.740
everything from supply
chain, to e-commerce,

00:45:14.740 --> 00:45:17.810
to product development,
to marketing--

00:45:17.810 --> 00:45:20.562
because it's one BigQuery
data set, a number of users

00:45:20.562 --> 00:45:22.270
can do a lot of
different things with it.

00:45:22.270 --> 00:45:25.480
And because we can rearrange
blue hexagons pretty quickly,

00:45:25.480 --> 00:45:29.230
we can be agile and respond to
our business needs out of that.

00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:31.300
The first set of metrics
we looked at, though,

00:45:31.300 --> 00:45:33.310
were looking at
consumer sentiment

00:45:33.310 --> 00:45:36.750
across a variety of
brands and topics,

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:39.070
doing the entity
extraction from that,

00:45:39.070 --> 00:45:42.490
and then understanding both
the whitespace in products,

00:45:42.490 --> 00:45:45.640
but also being able
to respond quickly

00:45:45.640 --> 00:45:49.060
to the different complaints,
or the different feedback

00:45:49.060 --> 00:45:51.580
mechanisms, consumers
were providing to us.

00:45:51.580 --> 00:45:53.590
One of the really cool
things we could do--

00:45:53.590 --> 00:45:55.750
in one case, we
had a user that had

00:45:55.750 --> 00:45:58.900
a team that spoke two
languages, but needed to operate

00:45:58.900 --> 00:46:00.520
in 10 different countries.

00:46:00.520 --> 00:46:02.470
It was very easy to
add a natural language

00:46:02.470 --> 00:46:06.670
processing, a translation
API, on top of that

00:46:06.670 --> 00:46:09.550
and then quickly use that team
to get their arms around much

00:46:09.550 --> 00:46:10.738
bigger business.

00:46:10.738 --> 00:46:12.280
We really feel like
where we're going

00:46:12.280 --> 00:46:15.910
to fight in this space
with an army of capability

00:46:15.910 --> 00:46:18.760
that we can quickly give
our teams distributed

00:46:18.760 --> 00:46:19.970
around the world.

00:46:19.970 --> 00:46:22.700
We can also incorporate
new data sets very quickly.

00:46:22.700 --> 00:46:26.380
So a source like Twitter,
much more structured API,

00:46:26.380 --> 00:46:28.000
directly bring it
in, correlate it

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:29.538
to other data sets
that are there.

00:46:29.538 --> 00:46:31.330
When I was in the
government, we were doing

00:46:31.330 --> 00:46:32.650
really cool things with tweets.

00:46:32.650 --> 00:46:35.470
We could geolocate
based on text alone.

00:46:35.470 --> 00:46:38.890
And at Colgate, we're finding
a lot of great information's

00:46:38.890 --> 00:46:41.400
coming from the correlation
of disparate data

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:44.440
sets and the ability to
bring data out of the tools

00:46:44.440 --> 00:46:49.480
that we have into a common
construct, and through using ML

00:46:49.480 --> 00:46:51.370
and code where we need to.

00:46:51.370 --> 00:46:54.460
Also, using Tableau or
the Google Data Studio

00:46:54.460 --> 00:46:56.253
has been the most
helpful for users

00:46:56.253 --> 00:46:58.420
that don't have a coding
background, because they've

00:46:58.420 --> 00:47:00.878
been able to rearrange and
build their own dashboards.

00:47:00.878 --> 00:47:02.920
They don't get stuck with
a static dashboard that

00:47:02.920 --> 00:47:04.690
presupposes the question.

00:47:04.690 --> 00:47:07.150
And they have the ability
to really explore the data

00:47:07.150 --> 00:47:08.410
that they're working with.

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:10.300
Just to show the
example architecture

00:47:10.300 --> 00:47:13.150
that we used for this
sentiment analysis,

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:17.800
you see a common repetition
of the Google Cloud Storage

00:47:17.800 --> 00:47:19.510
capability throughout that.

00:47:19.510 --> 00:47:22.510
That's because storing data
raw is an important lesson

00:47:22.510 --> 00:47:25.130
learned in the past and
very relevant in this space,

00:47:25.130 --> 00:47:26.150
as well.

00:47:26.150 --> 00:47:28.358
When we are going out
and acquiring data

00:47:28.358 --> 00:47:29.900
from a number of
sources, if we might

00:47:29.900 --> 00:47:33.110
start on an e-commerce site,
ingest all the reviews.

00:47:33.110 --> 00:47:35.300
But we might then need
to go to something

00:47:35.300 --> 00:47:38.270
like a Kickstarter or an
Etsy to see where trends

00:47:38.270 --> 00:47:41.750
might be starting and to follow
them down to their very source.

00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:43.520
For that, we need a
lot of flexibility

00:47:43.520 --> 00:47:46.610
to direct what I tell our
leadership is a spotlight

00:47:46.610 --> 00:47:50.210
to go out and get us
the data that we need.

00:47:50.210 --> 00:47:52.550
One of the most important
things is storage is cheap,

00:47:52.550 --> 00:47:55.040
people are expensive,
and time is critical.

00:47:55.040 --> 00:47:56.900
With that in mind, we
store everything raw

00:47:56.900 --> 00:47:59.330
because we have to go back
to the original source data

00:47:59.330 --> 00:48:02.480
and extract extra information
that we can find there.

00:48:02.480 --> 00:48:05.360
Once it's there, we use
Cloud Pub/Sub, also setting

00:48:05.360 --> 00:48:07.050
the stage for
streaming capabilities,

00:48:07.050 --> 00:48:10.230
particularly important in
a supply chain environment.

00:48:10.230 --> 00:48:13.430
But here, it allows for batch
processing for whenever reviews

00:48:13.430 --> 00:48:16.880
arrive, Pub/Sub kicks
off a cloud function

00:48:16.880 --> 00:48:18.740
that then scores
all the metrics,

00:48:18.740 --> 00:48:21.170
produces all the
insight around the data,

00:48:21.170 --> 00:48:24.200
pushes it back into another
cloud storage bucket,

00:48:24.200 --> 00:48:26.300
and eventually into BigQuery.

00:48:26.300 --> 00:48:29.000
The reason we do this
temporary storage

00:48:29.000 --> 00:48:30.860
along these different
stages is because it

00:48:30.860 --> 00:48:34.040
allows us to store data raw,
which our data scientists can

00:48:34.040 --> 00:48:37.620
go back then and extract
extra information from.

00:48:37.620 --> 00:48:41.900
And then finally, once it's in
BigQuery, from a CSO security

00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:43.840
leader perspective,
it's very helpful.

00:48:43.840 --> 00:48:45.590
It's an append-only database.

00:48:45.590 --> 00:48:47.310
I can give my teams
a lot of freedom.

00:48:47.310 --> 00:48:50.300
And by working closely with
Google's security teams

00:48:50.300 --> 00:48:53.360
I can distribute this capability
across the company really

00:48:53.360 --> 00:48:57.230
quickly and do so knowing that
the security is in a construct

00:48:57.230 --> 00:49:01.550
that my SOC is monitoring and
my teams can use very quickly.

00:49:01.550 --> 00:49:04.140
So I'd love to talk about
the details of this more.

00:49:04.140 --> 00:49:06.380
I'd love to hear
stories about how others

00:49:06.380 --> 00:49:10.910
are using this capability
to quickly move, understand,

00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:13.470
and act on all the
information that's out there.

00:49:13.470 --> 00:49:14.720
And thank you for your time.

00:49:14.720 --> 00:49:17.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

